<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	**APP-26.** The macOS entitlements: the iOS set, plus the one thing that
	makes the LGPL promise in `AboutPane` true rather than aspirational.

	`project.yml` selects this file for `sdk=macosx*` only, the same way it
	conditions LD_RUNPATH_SEARCH_PATHS, and `Currawong.entitlements` stays the
	iOS set. Two files rather than one with a macOS-only key in it, because
	`com.apple.security.cs.*` keys are meaningless on iOS and an entitlement the
	platform does not know is a provisioning mismatch waiting to happen on the
	one platform whose signing is not ours to debug.

	Keep the keychain group here identical to the iOS file's. It is the same
	app; a divergence would present as the secret store working on one platform.
	-->
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)au.charlesmartin.currawong</string>
	</array>

	<!--
	**Why this key is here, and what it does not do.**

	LGPL-2.1 §6 permits distributing a work that uses the library only if the
	recipient may modify the library and relink. LP-4 answers the linking half
	by shipping Codec2 as its own dynamic framework. This key answers a
	narrower question: under the hardened runtime that notarisation requires,
	macOS enforces *library validation* and refuses to load nested code signed
	by another team. This turns that check off for this app.

	Be accurate about the size of that. The substitution procedure the app
	documents — replace the framework, re-sign the application, launch — was
	measured working on 2026-08-24, and it does not need this key: re-signing
	the application ad-hoc makes the app and the framework the same signer, and
	drops the hardened runtime with it, so library validation never applies.
	What this key adds is the case where the two signers differ: a
	Codec2.framework signed by somebody else inside an application re-signed
	without it. That is a real way to relink and it would otherwise fail.

	So: not the basis on which the Mac build is distributable — dynamic linking
	is that — but one more route to substitution left open, at the cost of this
	app's own library validation. Taken deliberately. It does not close OQ-6
	for the App Store build, where the bundle cannot be modified at all and no
	entitlement helps.

	Related and separate: `keychain-access-groups` above is a *restricted*
	entitlement and needs a provisioning profile embedded in the bundle.
	Without one the kernel kills the app on launch with no message, after
	codesign has called the signature valid. The `com.apple.security.cs.*`
	keys need no profile. See docs/LICENSING.md for the measurements.

	The hardened runtime is turned on by the release script as a signing option
	rather than here, because that is what it is and not an entitlement.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
